        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          You do not need a stud finder to find a stud. Wall framing follows a predictable layout, usually 16 inches between stud centers, and almost everything fastened to a wall, from outlet boxes to baseboards to the drywall itself, is fastened to those studs. Find one reliable clue, measure from it, and confirm with a second method before you drill. That is how carpenters did it for generations, and it is still the most dependable way to hang something heavy when there is no gadget in the drawer.
        </p>

        <div id="how-walls-are-built" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Walls Are Framed, and Why That Helps
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every method in this guide depends on one fact: studs are not placed at random. In most North American homes built in the last several decades, wall studs are spaced 16 inches on center, meaning 16 inches from the middle of one stud to the middle of the next. Some walls use 24 inches on center, which is common in newer energy-efficient framing, in some interior partition walls and in garages. Older homes can be less regular, which we cover in the plaster section.
          </p>

          <p>
            A standard stud is a 2x4 or 2x6, and its actual face width is 1.5 inches, not 2. That narrow target is why guesswork fails. Miss by an inch and your screw goes into hollow drywall. So the goal is never just to find a stud, but to find its center, or at least both edges. When you locate one edge, the center is three quarters of an inch further in, and that is where a screw should go.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drywall is built around the same layout. Sheets are 4 feet wide, which is three stud bays at 16 inches or two at 24 inches, so vertical sheet edges land on studs. The drywall is then screwed or nailed to every stud it crosses, typically every 12 to 16 inches up the wall. Those fasteners are what a magnet finds, and the seams and screw dimples are what a low-angle flashlight reveals. The wall is full of evidence once you know the pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical approach is to collect clues in order of reliability, establish one stud with confidence, measure along to find the next ones, then confirm the exact spot before committing. Each method below has a failure mode, so the rule is two independent confirmations for anything heavy. If you hang things often, a dedicated tool is quicker, and our <a href="/best-stud-finder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">stud finder buying guide</a> covers which ones are actually accurate.
          </p>

