        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Finding a stud without a stud finder comes down to reading the wall. Start from something that is fastened to framing, measure along at 16 or 24 inches, confirm with a magnet on the drywall screws, and finish with a small test hole behind whatever you are hanging.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Respect the exceptions, including old-work boxes, irregular plaster walls and steel studs, and keep clear of the zones above and below electrical boxes. And if you find yourself doing this every weekend, a good stud finder is a small price for getting it right the first time.
          </p>
